WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.480
MUSIC

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:37.200
The polar regions are the coldest, most extreme places on our planet.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:47.800
Winds reach 200 miles an hour and temperatures drop as low as minus 70 degrees centigrade.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.080
Only the toughest can survive here.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:01:00.120
Over the course of a year, polar animals must overcome not just the cold,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.680
but the most extreme seasonal changes on Earth.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:17.280
In the high Arctic, winter is one long night that lasts for months.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:26.520
On these side slopes, beneath the snow, new lives are beginning.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:43.240
The cubs are born blind and tiny.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:59.920
An early birth is easier on the mother, who is barely awake.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:14.160
Despite her sleepiness, her instinct to nurse is overwhelming.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:32.960
The cubs' clucking calls stimulate her to produce milk.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.840
And what milk? It's nine times richer than her own,

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.440
and enables her to double their weight every few weeks.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:03:08.800
The sun is returning after an absence of nearly four months,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.360
and its warmth starts to transform this magical ice world.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.640
Spring has arrived.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.360
The greatest seasonal change on our planet is now underway.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:51.240
Antarctica is still locked in ice and surrounded by a frozen ocean.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.520
Nonetheless, there are signs of spring.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:06.080
The daily penguins are arriving, just the males.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.560
They've spent five months at sea, where it's warmer than it is on land,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:21.360
and now they're in a hurry, for spring will be short.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.760
They have travelled 6,000 miles across the ocean

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.960
since leaving their colony last year, and now they're returning to breed.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.280
They cannot lay their eggs on ice, for they would freeze,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.880
so they have to come here, where there is bare rock.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.960
Over the coming months, the few parts of Antarctica that are ice-free

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:57.520
will be the stage on which five million adalies will build their nests.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.360
To construct one, they need pebbles, and without a good-looking nest,

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:08.600
a male will be unable to attract a female when they, at last, arrive.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:15.760
An impressive property demonstrates your worth as a mate.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.920
It takes stones of all shapes and sizes to build a decent nest,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:28.520
and finding ones that are just right is not easy.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:35.520
So some penguins turn to a life of crime.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:15.880
The one who has been robbed seems unaware that the thief is just over his shoulder,

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:28.080
and looking for more.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.800
The thief's nest is coming along nicely,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.960
probably because he keeps a particularly sharp lookout for robbers.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:46.040
After all, it takes one to know one.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.320
By fair means or foul, the males must finish their nests quickly

00:06:50.320 --> 00:07:08.720
if they're to raise a family over the short Antarctic summer.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:13.760
In the north of our planet, the weak sunshine is a welcome relief

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:21.640
after the months of darkness.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:31.920
Finally, it's time for polar bear families to emerge from their dens.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.480
This is not the easiest place to take your first steps,

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:48.400
and the little ones will need plenty of encouragement from their mother.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:08:03.280
If you can raise all three to independence, it will be a rare achievement.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:11.400
During the months underground, all they've known is their mother.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:26.680
Now the big wide world holds all sorts of challenges for these brave young explorers.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:37.280
The mother leads her new family out over the frozen sea to hunt for seals.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:44.120
But the spring melt is already underway.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.920
All across the Arctic, the sun's warming effect is increasing.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:55.800
Sea ice is showing the first signs of weakening.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:03.200
In mainland, the northern rivers are still locked in ice.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:08.440
The frozen waterfalls are like dams, holding back billions of tons of fresh water

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:15.080
that has not moved for almost six months.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:25.560
The vast watershed lies motionless, but as spring advances, it begins to stir.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.720
The frozen waterfalls start to weaken.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:38.200
Above them, the pressure is mounting.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:46.920
Now from high above, whole sections can be seen to be on the move.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:10:04.920
The waterfalls are straining to hold back the force that is building up above them.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:34.840
The dam bursts and the river is unleashed.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:39.800
These vast floods accelerate the breakup.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:53.840
Soon an area of sea ice the size of Australia will vanish from the Arctic Ocean.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:06.240
The spring melt breaks the ice for visitors.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.280
The unicorns of the north are on a mission to reach the new fishing grounds in the bays

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:23.800
that have been frozen up all winter, but are now opening up.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:30.400
To get to them, the narwhals must travel down leads, temporary cracks in the ice.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:39.240
But these new roads could close at any time, cutting off the air that they need to breathe.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:44.720
The road narrows until there's barely room for one-way traffic.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:55.260
Then a surprise, narwhals coming from the other direction.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.240
It's a standoff.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:10.240
Each team faces an armory of sharp tusks.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:32.000
Finally, one side concedes and everyone continues in the same direction.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:44.920
The melting sea ice reveals open ocean and the changes on land are no less dramatic.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:50.440
Beneath the snow, life is already stirring.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:56.120
The Arctic tundra is unveiled.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:23.640
Water that has been locked in ice all winter once again flows freely.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:50.600
A landscape that was only white now bursts with colour.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:14:04.120
Ice begin arriving from the south and suddenly the tundra is alive with new families.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:20.840
These Arctic wolf cubs are just over a month old and always looking out for their next

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:21.840
meal.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.760
Their parents have been hunting.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:29.920
Their offerings are devoured instantly.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:40.760
Care is a mainstay of the tundra diet and one the cubs seem particularly keen on.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:46.800
Uneaten food is usually hidden for leaner times but there will be no leftovers today.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:15:01.120
The cubs are growing fast and are always hungry.

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:07.720
The good times are certainly back but these white wolves remind us of the Arctic's less

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:09.480
welcoming side.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:16.780
Their coats are pale to conceal them during the long snowy winter.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:23.760
It's easy to forget that one month ago this land was a barren white desert.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:31.300
But finding a decent meal here is never easy.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:36.520
The parents travel up to 80 miles in a day in search of more substantial prey for their

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:50.560
growing family.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:16:03.920
Muskoxen are immensely powerful and their sharp horns can kill but a calf is more vulnerable.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:30.080
The two wolves work together to split the herd and isolate their victim.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:56.480
It seems that the wolf cubs will at last eat well.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:06.920
But the herd regroups.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:22.880
The cavalry ride to the rescue.

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:28.280
The wolves cannot penetrate the wall of horns.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:33.720
The herd are protecting their calf.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:44.320
For the muskoxen it's all for one and one for all.

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:49.880
It's a struggle for all polar animals to feed themselves and their families in the brief

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:54.360
Arctic summer.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:59.600
But there is one tiny creature of the tundra that has found an astounding solution to the

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:02.120
shortness of the season.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:06.080
The woolly bear caterpillar.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:12.280
It's always the first insect to appear after the snow's retreat.

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:18.280
The caterpillar then eats as fast as it can and indeed it must for this far north summer

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:28.200
only lasts a few weeks.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:34.160
The days shorten only too soon but the caterpillar has not yet got enough reserves to transform

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:36.620
itself into a moth.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:44.460
It can't leave the Arctic for it can't fly so it settles down beneath a rock.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:53.240
The sun's warmth rapidly dwindles.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:58.160
Beneath the rock the caterpillar is out of the wind but the cold penetrates deep into

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:01.160
the ground.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:04.020
Soon its heart stops beating.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:09.280
It ceases to breathe and its body starts to freeze.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:36.600
But its gut then its blood.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:46.880
After four months of darkness the Arctic begins to thaw and the caterpillar rises from the

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:55.160
dead.

00:19:55.160 --> 00:20:00.280
By the time the first shoots of willow appear in the early spring the woolly bear is already

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:02.680
eating.

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:08.000
But no matter how fast the woolly bear eats it will not have time to gather enough food

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:32.080
this year either and the cold closes in once again.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:45.960
Year after year the caterpillar slows down in the autumn and then freezes solid.

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:53.480
But eventually a very special summer arrives.

00:20:53.480 --> 00:21:01.760
This one will be its last.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:07.360
It's now 14 years old the world's oldest caterpillar.

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:10.280
Its remaining days now become frantic.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:16.600
It starts to weave a silk cocoon.

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:22.640
Inside its body is changing into one that can fly and search.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:38.400
Things that will be crucial in the days ahead.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:42.240
All across the Arctic moths are emerging.

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:47.840
After completing their 14 year preparation they now have just a few days to find a partner

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:55.160
and mate.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:22:07.280
No life illustrates more vividly the struggle to survive in this most seasonal of places.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:14.600
In a matter of weeks the north will be frozen over once again.

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:29.760
At the southern end of our planet Antarctica is still surrounded by sea ice.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:36.600
The Antarctic spring arrives first at the outer islands.

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:41.360
Although the beaches of South Georgia are now ice free the king penguins face a new

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:42.360
challenge.

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:50.600
Their peaceful waterfront has turned into an obstacle course of blubber.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:56.200
The elephant seals have arrived.

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:05.280
The beach masters authority is being challenged.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:09.840
This rival means business.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:27.480
The beach master owns the females here and must fight to keep them.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:43.120
The beach master himself weighs four tons but this rival is his equal.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:24:02.540
The

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:30.380
The

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:36.300
beach master has won the first battle, but he may have to defend his harem every hour

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:41.860
for the next month. If he can stay master of his beach for this period, many of the

00:24:41.860 --> 00:25:00.340
young born here next year will be his.

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:04.260
In summer, the southern ocean bursts with life.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:38.260
No bird is more at home in water, and they are masterful surfers.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:51.420
Penguins are found only in the southern hemisphere. They can't fly, but they don't

00:25:51.420 --> 00:26:03.660
need to. There are no polar bears here.

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:10.100
These are gentoo penguins. Each spring they come ashore to lay their eggs and rear their

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:19.220
young. Their hungry chicks demand so much seafood that both parents have to go fishing.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:40.020
And fishing can be dangerous.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:27:06.940
The southern sea lion. It uses the speed of a breaking wave to catch up with the gentoo's.

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:12.780
Sea lions normally eat fish, so he's used to catching streamlined swimmers. But the

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:23.460
gentoo is even more than his match out at sea. He must change tactics. Perhaps it will

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:40.540
be easier in the shallows.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:28:02.060
But no, it seems penguins are uncatchable in water. How about on land? The penguins

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:07.660
wings, so powerful for swimming, are of no help when it comes to running. Now, surely

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:31.260
the sea lion has a chance. But on the beach, both are like fish out of water.

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:47.180
Rarely do hunter and hunted play their roles with so little skill. The outcome is anyone's

00:28:47.180 --> 00:29:02.620
guess.

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:29.900
Every summer, over 40 million penguins take to the southern ocean to feed. They're joined

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:42.460
by thousands of whales. Minkies are the most numerous. They all come here to harvest the

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:54.940
richest ocean on earth. Southern humpbacks have traveled 4,000 miles from the equator

00:29:54.940 --> 00:30:05.940
to get here in time for the Antarctic summer. Each year, the sea ice that surrounds Antarctica

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:11.620
melts, effectively halving the size of the continent and exposing rich waters around

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:23.180
its coastline. It's the most spectacular seasonal change occurring anywhere on our planet.

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:30.420
The remnants of the sea ice are occupied by sunbathing seals that have been here all

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:39.220
winter. But new arrivals are following the retreating ice edge, and they have come here

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:55.100
to hunt. Killer whales, the ocean's top predator. Killers are like wolves, for they will hunt

00:30:55.100 --> 00:31:00.860
animals far larger than themselves. But even smaller prey are a problem if you can't reach

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:15.260
them. The solution is teamwork. Swimming in perfect formation, they flick their tails

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:30.700
in unison and create a wave that cracks the ice. They regroup and assess the damage. A

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:43.700
more powerful wave is needed. The ice flow is breaking up. Now they're close enough to

00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:50.380
get a good look at their target. The seal is a crab eater, sharp-toothed and feisty,

00:31:50.380 --> 00:32:04.940
not their favorite. The wolves of the sea move on in search of easier quarry. A whittle

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:14.940
seal, that's better. These are more docile and easier to tackle. The pod stays close

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:24.020
together and travels silently. This time they unleash a far more powerful wave and with

00:32:24.020 --> 00:32:36.700
astonishing accuracy. These big waves are not intended to break the ice, but to knock

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:55.100
the prey into the water and they rarely fail.

00:32:55.100 --> 00:33:04.100
Keep an eye out!

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:12.220
Ok we're done!

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:28.900
The seal is now where the killers wanted.

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:33.060
But the hunt is far from over.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:39.100
They need to grab their prey by the tail while avoiding its snapping jaws.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:47.020
Only then will they be able to pull it down and drown it.

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:59.420
Side swipes create violent underwater turbulence, a new tactic.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:12.140
Blowing bubbles gives cover for others to lunge at the seal's tail.

00:34:12.140 --> 00:34:20.900
Somehow the seal manages to reach a tiny ice floe.

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:28.260
The killers could easily grab it, but now this seems to become a game.

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:43.980
The seal's life hangs on a roll of the ice.

00:34:43.980 --> 00:35:05.660
Yet again, the pod joins forces to dislodge the seal.

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:12.340
The seal sees a chance to escape.

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:17.500
Exhausted, it no longer has the energy to pull itself to safety.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:28.780
And the killers are moving in.

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:42.220
Game over.

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:48.340
As the Antarctic summer draws to an end, visitors that rely on the brief flush of food will

00:35:48.340 --> 00:35:56.980
soon be forced north by the return of the sea ice.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:06.100
For the largest land predator, the sea ice cannot return soon enough.

00:36:06.100 --> 00:36:14.180
The end of the Arctic summer, and the sun hasn't set for three months.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:28.500
It's hard to imagine the bitter cold will soon return.

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:34.020
Some will welcome the chills of autumn, but for most, time is running out before they

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:43.060
will have to retreat to the south.

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:48.820
The shorter days and colder nights trigger a dramatic change in the willows and blueberry

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:50.180
bushes.

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:55.500
They stop producing the green pigment that harnesses the sun's energy, and red and yellow

00:36:55.500 --> 00:37:07.980
pigments build up in their leaves.

00:37:07.980 --> 00:37:33.220
The tundra blazes with color, and the whole landscape is transformed.

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:35.460
The sound of autumn.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:45.100
The musk ox rut has begun.

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:51.180
This clash of polar titans could easily become a fight to the death as males battle over

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:53.180
females.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:38:09.420
A heavy skull and helmet of horn four inches thick provides some protection for the musk

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:13.020
ox's brain.

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:34.740
The impact is like a car crash at 30 miles an hour.

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:41.300
Over the next half hour, the advantage ebbs and flows as each tries to finish the duel.

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:56.380
If one can turn the other and gore its flank, the fight will be over.

00:38:56.380 --> 00:39:14.420
The stakes rise as the risk of injury or even death increases.

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:25.900
This male is outmaneuvered, and the champion returns to claim his females.

00:39:25.900 --> 00:39:31.820
By now, most animals have migrated south, but the musk ox with their thick coats will

00:39:31.820 --> 00:39:43.340
stay and face the approaching winter.

00:39:43.340 --> 00:39:51.500
The moisture in the air freezes, and hoarfrost decorates the leaves.

00:39:51.500 --> 00:40:12.180
Ice crystals grow like shards of glass on every twig.

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:17.820
As the sun's influence continues to dwindle here in the north, the freeze pushes into

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:30.340
the tree line, spreading over one fifth of the planet.

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:46.100
The northern forests lock down for winter.

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:56.820
The last running water freezes, and cathedrals of ice are formed.

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:07.140
The great waterfalls of the north shut down.

00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:13.380
The stage is now set for a magical event.

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:17.820
The formation of snowflakes.

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:31.260
Cold air meets warm air from the south, and the moisture it carries crystallizes.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:44.140
Snowflakes will have a six-fold symmetry, but no two have ever been found with exactly

00:41:44.140 --> 00:41:45.140
the same shape.

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:49.700
Each snowflake is water waiting to be released in spring.

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:55.900
For this reason, snow is the lifeblood of these silent forests, and all that live here

00:41:55.900 --> 00:42:05.380
depend on it in one way or another.

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:26.900
Snowflakes will then, like the great grey owl, appear in spring for the boom times,

00:42:26.900 --> 00:42:45.860
as white snow castle rises again, passing through the multip Ihasmade.

00:42:45.860 --> 00:43:02.300
Stooped, shrouded figures bear the weight of winter's heavy snows.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:44.980
The heavy snows make it hard to get around, even for the largest and most powerful wolves

00:43:44.980 --> 00:43:55.220
in the world. Today they are setting out to hunt. The pack is 25 strong, a sign that the prey they

00:43:55.220 --> 00:44:01.380
are seeking is formidable, the largest land animal in North America, bison.

00:44:01.380 --> 00:44:18.180
The bison form a defensive circle around their young, horns pointing outwards.

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:37.100
The wolves need a bison to break rank, but the tables are turning and now the wolves have to

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:46.220
retreat. The pack focus their attention on the rear of the herd and the bison begin to panic.

00:45:07.100 --> 00:45:32.220
A young bison falls behind. Even this yearling dwarfs the wolves.

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:51.300
Running head down, the herds only thought is escape. A stroke of luck for the wolves.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:46:11.140
The kill will feed the pack for several days, but then they will have to resume the chase.

00:46:11.140 --> 00:46:29.860
At the frozen ends of our planet, the struggle for survival never eases. For a few, the snow is

00:46:29.860 --> 00:46:43.900
an ally. Voles stay active throughout the winter, moving between pockets of perfectly

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:50.620
refrigerated food in a network of tunnels. These tunnels are a sanctuary from predators.

00:46:50.620 --> 00:47:13.460
Except one. The least weasel is a tiny hunter and the vole's nemesis. Its body is exactly the same

00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:17.700
width as a vole's, so there's nowhere a vole can go that a weasel can't follow.

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:51.860
The weasel's long, slender shape is perfect for hunting in tunnels, but the worst possible

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:59.460
shape for staying warm, so they need a special way of doing that. She plucks the fur from its

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:15.700
body tuft by tuft. And now she puts it all together to make a cosy blanket under which to

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:39.420
sleep in her den. A hundred miles above the earth, the aurora lights up the sky.

00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:57.180
After travelling millions of miles across space, solar winds attracted by the magnetic pull of the

00:48:57.180 --> 00:49:06.860
poles collide with the earth's atmosphere. Trillions of charged particles dance across the sky.

00:49:06.860 --> 00:49:21.740
Above the Arctic, the aurora borealis, the northern lights. In the south, it's the aurora australis,

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:36.620
the southern lights, that bring light to Antarctica's long winter. These spectacular

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:44.100
light shows are only a tease. Solar energy may be, but no warmth that will help the emperor penguins.

00:49:44.100 --> 00:50:11.220
The male penguins have not eaten for months and have only each other for protection from hurricane

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:21.940
force winds. They have been deserted by the females, left to incubate their eggs throughout

00:50:21.940 --> 00:50:44.980
the worst winter on earth. The emperors are not entirely alone. The weddle seal,

00:50:44.980 --> 00:50:57.780
the only mammal to remain here throughout the winter. Beneath the ice, they are beyond the

00:50:57.780 --> 00:51:13.340
reach of the bitter winter winds. The sea is minus two degrees centigrade, a warm bath compared to

00:51:13.340 --> 00:51:27.380
the conditions overhead. The roof of ice insulates this world from the wild fluctuations above.

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:47.740
The temperature down here has barely changed for 25 million years. There could hardly be a

00:51:47.740 --> 00:52:07.940
greater contrast to the bleak windswept world just above. But there is a constant danger here.

00:52:07.940 --> 00:52:19.700
Swirling patterns in the water reveal its presence. They're made by brine,

00:52:19.700 --> 00:52:32.780
super concentrated saltwater. It's a warning. New sea ice forming above leaves behind brine

00:52:32.780 --> 00:52:45.220
that is so extremely salty it sinks rapidly. As it descends, the seawater around it freezes

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:59.620
instantly and forms a sheath of ice, a brineicle that grows downwards towards the seafloor. Winter

00:52:59.620 --> 00:53:26.260
is reaching down from the cold world above. As it touches the seafloor, it kills whatever

00:53:26.260 --> 00:53:45.340
living thing it contacts by encasing it in a tomb of ice. As calm returns beneath the ice,

00:53:45.340 --> 00:54:03.340
a dramatic change is coming to the world above.

00:54:15.340 --> 00:54:25.700
The sun returns to Antarctica. The longest night on earth has ended and winter begins to give way

00:54:25.700 --> 00:54:53.500
to spring. Female emperor penguins, after four months feeding at sea, they're returning sleek

00:54:53.500 --> 00:54:57.220
and fat.

00:55:23.500 --> 00:55:34.980
Penguins, it seems, can fly after all. But a winter at sea has left them a little out of practice.

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:49.700
There is no time to waste. Far away, the males are waiting. Reunited after three months apart.

00:56:49.700 --> 00:57:10.780
The reward for the females return, a first glimpse of her chick. A task that began in autumn has

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:19.420
been completed. Despite the huge odds against it, the precious chick has survived the winter

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:35.380
and is now with its mother. And she has food. The chick's first fresh meal. The females mission

00:57:35.380 --> 00:57:55.900
is complete. For those emperor penguins that survive, the worst is over for this year. There

00:57:55.900 --> 00:58:09.100
will soon be abundant food for everyone. The emperors have taken on the polar winter and won.

00:58:09.100 --> 00:58:17.260
The gamble has paid off. All other animals escaped, only they remained with their eggs.

00:58:17.260 --> 00:58:24.660
And it's they who will benefit most from the rich southern spring. Their epic journey is

00:58:24.660 --> 00:58:26.660
complete.
